I see! At line 696:
```c
memset(MI->flat_insn->detail, 0, offsetof(cs_detail, wasm)+sizeof(cs_wasm));
```

This zeros out the `cs_detail` struct up to and including the `wasm` field. The `wasm` field contains `operands` (a pointer), which is set to NULL. Then when `read_varuint32` is called, it allocates new operands.

But wait - the `cs_detail` struct has fields AFTER the union too. Let me check:

```c
typedef struct cs_detail {
    uint16_t regs_read[16];
    uint8_t regs_read_count;
    uint16_t regs_write[20];
    uint8_t regs_write_count;
    uint8_t groups[8];
    uint8_t groups_count;
    union {
        ...
        cs_wasm wasm;
        ...
    };
} cs_detail;
```

The `offsetof(cs_detail, wasm)` gives the offset of the `wasm` field within the union. And `sizeof(cs_wasm)` is the size of the `cs_wasm` struct. So the memset zeros from the start of the struct to `offsetof(cs_detail, wasm) + sizeof(cs_wasm)`.

But `cs_wasm` is:
```c
typedef struct cs_wasm {
    uint8_t op_count;
    cs_wasm_op *operands;
} cs_wasm;
```

So `sizeof(cs_wasm)` = 16 bytes (8 for the pointer + alignment). The memset zeros from the start of `cs_detail` to `offsetof(cs_detail, wasm) + 16`. This covers the `wasm` field completely.

